.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH git-veil-unhide 1  "git-veil-unhide " 
.SH NAME
git\-veil\-unhide \- Decrypt one tracked file back to plaintext and delete its ciphertext
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBgit\-veil unhide\fR [\fB\-\-email\fR] [\fB\-\-remote\fR] [\fB\-\-key\-store\fR] [\fB\-\-passphrase\-stdin\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIFILE\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Decrypt one tracked file back to plaintext and delete its ciphertext
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-email\fR \fI<EMAIL>\fR
Your email address
.TP
\fB\-\-remote\fR \fI<REMOTE>\fR [default: origin]
Git remote name
.TP
\fB\-\-key\-store\fR \fI<KEY_STORE>\fR
Key store directory (default: $HOME/.git\-veil)
.TP
\fB\-\-passphrase\-stdin\fR
Read the passphrase for a passphrase\-protected private key from stdin (exactly one line). Wins over the GITVEIL_PASSPHRASE environment variable; never pass a passphrase as a CLI argument
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fIFILE\fR>
File to unhide
.SH EXTRA
Decrypts one tracked file\*(Aqs in\-place <name>.secret ciphertext back to
the plaintext path and deletes the ciphertext — the inverse of hide for a
single file. The keyring signature is verified against the pinned trusted
key first; the file must be tracked and its ciphertext present. Typical
round trip: unhide to edit, then hide to re\-encrypt.

EXAMPLES
  $ git\-veil unhide .env
  $ git\-veil unhide .env \-\-email alice@example.com
  $ git\-veil unhide .env \-\-passphrase\-stdin < pass.txt
